    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. can you go into more detail on how we should handle edge cases where the UI elements might look okay but actually break for screen reader users? like when elements overlap or some stuff just doesn't announce right? how do we track those kinds of bugs?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When UI elements overlap or fail to announce correctly, you're dealing with accessibility bugs that are often more subtle than a simple contrast failure. These are tricky, as they are often related to the underlying DOM structure or how the native mobile accessibility APIs interpret the layout. You need to track these bugs not just as \"broken,\" but as \"contextually inaccessible,\" which is a more precise term for your audit documentation.\n\nTo track these edge cases, you should use a combination of automated testing tools (for basic structural checks) and, more importantly, manual testing using actual screen readers (like VoiceOver on iOS and TalkBack on Android). You must test in various states—focused, unfocused, error states, and when elements are partially obscured. If a user cannot logically follow the flow, it’s a failure, even if the colors look fine on the screen.",
      "source": "target",
