      "role": "user",
      "content": "yeah that makes sense. it's just that some of these bugs feel so random, like they're totally fine one minute and then they're just sabotaging the user the next. it's making the dev team pretty frustrated trying to hunt them down. any advice on how to manage that frustration or keep the momentum going when the bugs are that elusive?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When bugs are elusive, it often means the issue isn't a simple coding mistake, but rather a complex interaction between the code, the operating system, and the accessibility tree. To manage the dev team's frustration, I suggest framing these elusive bugs not as \"random failures,\" but as \"complex environmental interactions.\"\n\nKeep momentum by breaking down the testing into hyper-specific scenarios, like \"Test Focus Order on Modal Pop-up while Network is Slow.\" This precision helps isolate the variable, which is much more productive than chasing a vague \"it sometimes fails.\" Remember, when dealing with accessibility, the user experience is the ultimate test, and sometimes the most robust solution is to simplify the interaction design itself.",
      "source": "target",
